et ... in project Directory.Build.targets with target ... from Microsoft.Build.Tasks.Git.targets\". That means the SDK target is overriding MY target, not the other way around! So Directory.Build.targets is imported BEFORE Microsoft.Build.Tasks.Git.targets.\n\nThis is because in .NET SDK projects, Directory.Build.targets is imported early, and then SDK targets are imported. So my override gets overridden by the SDK.\n\nTo override SDK targets, I need to use a different approach. I can:\n1. Use `Directory.Build.props` to set `EnableSourceLink=false` and other properties - but we saw that didn't work.\n2. Use an `ImportAfter` target file in a known location.\n3. Set `GitRepositoryConfigurationScope` to `local` so it only reads local repo config, not global. But the local config might also be unreadable.\n4. Set `ContinuousIntegrationBuild` to `true` which automatically sets scope to local.\n\nActually, the comment in the targets file says: \"By default, the scope is restricted to 'local' when building in CI (ContinuousIntegrationBuild is true)\". If I set `ContinuousIntegrationBuild` to true, it will use local scope and not read global git config. But it might still fail if local config has issues? Actually, local scope reads only from `.git/config` in the repo, which should be readable.\n\nBut setting CI=true might have other effects like deterministic source paths. However, since I already disabled deterministic source paths, it might be OK.\n\nActually, the simplest fix: set `GitRepositoryConfigurationScope` to `local` in Directory.Build.props. This avoids reading `/Users/maui/.gitconfig`. Let me try that.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_efc717c7a001V1XYAWUo90a0H4","sessionID":"ses_103a00a69ffeXsNQ2547CvZLOb","messageID":"msg_efc7147ed001PUNT9be07mCgfW"}}
